<section xml:id="section-activity-slo2">
  <title>Activity for SLO-2</title>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-slo2-review">
    <title>Conceptual Review</title>
    <p>
      You can test your learning from the video (and your own
      background knowledge) with the following review questions.
      Conceptual questions on quizes may be taken from these review
      questions. 
    </p>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why do we need a unique rule for inverse derivatives?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          None of the other rules help directly with an inverse
          derivative. The inverse of a function is not an algebriac
          manipulation like exponents or multiplication, nor it is
          simply a composition for the chain rule. It is a unique
          operation, so a unique rule is required. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          How does the chain rule help to calculate the derivatives of
          inverse functions.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          An inverse function satisfies this equation.
          <md>
            f \circ f^{-1} (x) = x 
          </md>
          This is a composition, so I can apply the chain to
          differentiate the left side. Out of that chain rule, I can
          isolate the derivative of the inverse function.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsection-activity-inverse-derivatives">
    <title>Calculation Questions: Inverse Derivatives</title>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the rule for inverse derivative to prove this
          derivative.
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} \arccos x = \frac{-1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}} 
          </md>
          (You will need to use <m>\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1</m> in a
          clever way to simplify the expression.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The function I want, to use the rule, is <m>f(x) = \cos
          x</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x) = \arccos x</m>. Then I apply the
          rule, using the fact that <m>\frac{d}{dx} \cos x = - \sin
          x</m>. 
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} \arccos x = \frac{1}{-\sin(\arccos x)}
          </md>
          I can't directly simplify this, since <m>\sin (\arccos
          x)</m> can't be directly evaluated. However, I can write
          sine in terms of cosine. If I take the identity <m>\sin^2 x
          + \cos^2 x = 1</m> and solve for sine, I get this identity. 
          <md>
            \sin x = \sqrt{ 1 - \cos^2 x}
          </md>
          Now I can reply the sine in the previous expression with
          this square root. 
          <md> 
            \frac{-1}{\sqrt{1-\cos^2(\arccos x)}} =
            \frac{-1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}
          </md>
          After the replacement, I can evaluate cosine and inverse
          cosine<mdash/>I just get the variable. This completes the
          argument. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
    <activity>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the rule for inverse derivative to prove this
          derivative.
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} \arctan x = \frac{1}{1 + x^2} 
          </md>
          (You will need to use <m>\tan^2 x + 1 = \sec^2 x </m> in a
          clever way to simplify the expression.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The function I want, to use the rule, is <m>f(x) = \tan x
          x</m> with <m>f^{-1}(x) = \arctan x</m>. Then I apply the
          rule, using the fact that <m>\frac{d}{dx} \tan x = \sec^2
          x</m>. 
          <md>
            \frac{d}{dx} \arctan x = \frac{1}{\sec^2(\arccos x)}
          </md>
          I can't directly simplify this, again. But I can again use a
          trig identity. I use <m>\tan^2 x + 1 = \sec^2 x</m>. If have
          a secant squared in my expression, so I just isolate secant
          squared. 
          <md>
            \sec^2 x = 1 + \tan^2 x 
          </md>
          Now I can reply the secant squarred in the previous
          expression with this new expression.
          <md> 
            \frac{1}{1+\tan^2(\arctan x)} = \frac{1}{1+x^2}
          </md>
          After the replacement, I can evaluate tangent and arctanget
          to again just get the variable back. This completes the 
          argument. 
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </subsection>
</section>

